UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 39, Lesson 1: Hygiene and keeping our bodies healthy', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Hygiene means keeping ourselves and our surroundings clean so germs do not spread. Key habits: wash hands with soap and water for 20 seconds, cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue or your elbow, shower or bathe regularly, wear clean clothes and do not share towels, cups or hairbrushes. Staying home when sick protects other people.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA student sneezes into their hands and then touches a door handle. What is the problem and what should they do?\n\nFEEDBACK\nGerms from the sneeze are now on the handle and can spread. They should sneeze into a tissue or elbow, then wash their hands with soap and water for 20 seconds.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap. Washing with soap and water is best when hands are dirty; sanitiser helps when soap and water are not available.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate a hygiene poster showing five times to wash hands, the hand-washing steps and cough and sneeze etiquette. Time a 20-second wash and record it. Ask a family member to follow the steps and note any step they missed.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Hygiene means keeping ourselves and our surroundings clean so germs do not spread. Key habits: wash hands with soap and water for 20 seconds, cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue or your elbow, shower or bathe regularly, wear clean clothes and do not share towels, cups or hairbrushes. Staying home when sick protects other people.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create a hygiene poster showing five times to wash hands, the hand-washing steps and cough and sneeze etiquette. Time a 20-second wash and record it. Ask a family member to follow the steps and note any step they missed. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using hygiene on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap.. Correction: Germs from the sneeze are now on the handle and can spread.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap. Washing with soap and water is best when hands are dirty; sanitiser helps when soap and water are not available. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating hygiene on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with hygiene, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about hygiene with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for hygiene. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for hygiene. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Germs from the sneeze are now on the handle and can spread","Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"A student sneezes into their hands and then touches a door handle. What is the problem and what should they do?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for hygiene; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Hygiene means keeping ourselves and our surroundings clean so germs do not spread","Germs from the sneeze are now on the handle and can spread","Careful use of germs and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named germs and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap","Name germs and explain why it matters only, without linking it to hygiene","Apply hygiene to new material and name the germs and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies hygiene with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on hygiene. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap","Germs from the sneeze are now on the handle and can spread","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with hygiene","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Hygiene means keeping ourselves and our surroundings clean so germs do) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: when to wash hands","Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap","Name germs and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Create a hygiene poster showing five times to wash hands, the hand-washing steps and cough and sneeze etiquette","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies hygiene to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about hygiene with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating hygiene on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using hygiene on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for hygiene. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with hygiene, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap.. Correction: Germs from the sneeze are now on the handle and can spread.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w39-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise hygiene.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use hygiene with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify germs and explain why it matters.","I can practise hand washing independently on a new prompt.","I can notice cough and sneeze etiquette needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Hygiene means keeping ourselves and our surroundings clean so germs do not spread.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Hygiene means keeping ourselves and our surroundings clean so germs do not spread","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about hygiene with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: when to wash hands. Before eating, after the toilet, after coughing or sneezing, after playing outside and after touching animals. Mei coughs into her elbow, puts a used tissue in the bin and washes her hands with soap for 20 seconds. She stays home when she is sick so germs do not spread.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA student sneezes into their hands and then touches a door handle. What is the problem and what should they do?\n\nFEEDBACK\nGerms from the sneeze are now on the handle and can spread. They should sneeze into a tissue or elbow, then wash their hands with soap and water for 20 seconds.","explain":"Hygiene means keeping ourselves and our surroundings clean so germs do not spread. Key habits: wash hands with soap and water for 20 seconds, cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue or your elbow, shower or bathe regularly, wear clean clothes and do not share towels, cups or hairbrushes. Staying home when sick protects other people.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap. Washing with soap and water is best when hands are dirty; sanitiser helps when soap and water are not available. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Covering and washing.","caption":"Cover, then wash.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Sneeze</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">tissue or elbow</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Wash</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">soap, water, 20 seconds</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: when to wash hands. Before eating, after the toilet, after coughing or sneezing, after playing outside and afte Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: when to wash hands. Before eating, after the toilet, after coughing or sneezing, after playing outside and afte Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about hygiene with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: when to wash hands. Before eating, after the toilet, after coughing or sneezing, after playing outside and after touching animals. Mei coughs into her elbow, puts a used tissue in the bin and washes her hands with soap for 20 seconds. She stays home when she is sick so germs do not spread. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"When to wash and stay home.","caption":"Wash at key times.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">When</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">before eating, after toilet</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sick</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">stay home</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about hygiene with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap. Washing with soap and water is best when hands are dirty; sanitiser helps when soap and water are not available. Here is a more helpful example: Example: when to wash hands. Before eating, after the toilet, after coughing or sneezing, after playing outside and after touching animals. Mei coughs into her elbow, puts a used tissue in the bin and washes her hands with soap for 20 seconds. She stays home when she is sick so germs do not spread.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nGerms from the sneeze are now on the handle and can spread. They should sneeze into a tissue or elbow, then wash their hands with soap and water for 20 seconds.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking hand sanitiser is always better than soap. Washing with soap and water is best when hands are dirty; sanitiser helps when soap and water are not available. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about hygiene with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nHygiene means keeping ourselves and our surroundings clean so germs do not spread. Key habits: wash hands with soap and water for 20 seconds, cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue or your elbow, shower or bathe regularly, wear clean clothes and do not share towels, cups or hairbrushes. Staying home when sick protects other people. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 39, Lesson 1: Hygiene and keeping our bodies healthy","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Healthy habits","week":39,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: when to wash hands. Before eating, after the toilet, after coughing or sneezing, after playing outside and after touching animals. Mei coughs into her elbow, puts a used tissue in the bin and washes her hands with soap for 20 seconds. She stays home when she is sick so germs do not spread.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Covering and washing.","caption":"Cover, then wash.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Sneeze</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">tissue or elbow</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Wash</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">soap, water, 20 seconds</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"When to wash and stay home.","caption":"Wash at key times.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">When</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">before eating, after toilet</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sick</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">stay home</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w39-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
